e'd have a very  vigorous reaction remember hydrogen is  being produced when we react a metal  with an acid and we'll see loads and  loads of bubbles being produced here now  the test for hydrogen is called the  squeaky pop test now what you do is you  hold a lit splint over the test tube and  if there's a squeaky pop then we know  that hydrogen is present now this  squeaky pop would be really really loud  so we can test these with different  metals so the next one let's say we've  got aluminium there which is one down  the reactivity series then we'll have  zinc here iron here and in the final one  copper so what we'll find with each of  these let's say each of them is the same  strength of acid the same volume of acid  and the same mass of metal what we  should find is each one will have a  slightly less vigorous reaction with  hydrochloric acid so with this one it  will produce hydrogen at a very quick  rate there'll be a lot there loud  squeaky pop and a large one then this  one would have a slightly quieter  squeaky pop and slightly fewer bubbles  being produced at a slightly slower rate  and so on until we got to copper which  will have no reaction at all the reason  why copper does not react with  hydrochloric acid is because if we have  a look at the reactivity series copper  is below hydrogen so as a result it is  not react with with hydrochloric acid  now it's not just  acids  that metals can react with of really  really reactive metals such as potassium  and sodium these ones up here  they're so reactive that they react with  water now I'm just going to show you an  example using potassium so potassium  plus water makes potassium hydroxide and  hydrogen so just balance that quickly so  again  hydrogen is being produced so again we  could use a squeaky pop test to test for  this now some of these so zinc and iron  for example they don't react with water  they're not reactive enough to react  with water by itself but they can react  with steam so we have a 